    "case_id": "619",
    "case_summary": "A 33-year-old man with no medical history presented with a 1-day history of hemoptysis, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and dyspnea on exertion. He did not report orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, sore throat, runny nose, diarrhea, urinary symptoms, night sweats, or recent tuberculosis contact. He had received the Johnson & Johnson COVID vaccination approximately 4 weeks before presentation.\n\nInitial vitals:- Temperature: 98.9 °F- Heart rate: 76 beats/min- Blood pressure: 90/55 mm Hg- SpO₂: 99% on room airPhysical examination showed a lethargic man with unremarkable cardiopulmonary and abdominal examinations. Head and neck examination showed no lymphadenopathy. He developed a low-grade fever of 100 °F on day 1.\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Elevated inflammatory markers and serum creatinine- Serum troponin T: 0.153 µg/L- Pro–B-type natriuretic protein: 654.5 pg/mL- Serial electrocardiograms: No ischemic ST changes- Bronchoalveolar lavage: 3 consecutive samples with increasing bloody return, total red blood cell count of 9000/µLImaging Studies:- Chest radiograph: Bilateral multiple nodular infiltrates  Image Title: Figure 1A - Chest radiograph on presentation  Description: Shows bilateral multiple nodular infiltrates- CT pulmonary angiogram: Negative for pulmonary embolism, showed bilateral patchy nodular opacitiesCardiac Studies:- Echocardiogram findings:  - Left ventricular ejection fraction: 15%  - Diffuse left ventricular hypokinesis  - Dilated LV cavity but preserved right ventricular function- Right heart catheterization: Low filling pressures and low cardiac index",
